-- Generated by `php stone generate soft-delete`.
--
-- Run daily (wire into this platform's own cron/scheduler — the framework
-- does not ship a scheduler; see the generator's --help output). For every
-- configured table, physically DELETEs rows past their 7-day purge_after,
-- then marks the corresponding _deletion_archive rows purged=true, KEYED ON
-- THE SUBJECT_IDS THE DELETE ACTUALLY RETURNED (not a blanket
-- source_table+purge_after filter — see _purge_table_block.pgsql.template's
-- comment for why that distinction matters). The archive row itself is
-- NEVER deleted — see that table's comment.
--
-- Each table's purge runs in its OWN BEGIN/EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS sub-block:
-- ANY failure on one table (a platform-added FK with default RESTRICT, or a
-- platform-added trigger raising its own custom exception) is reported via
-- o_error for THAT table only and does not wedge or roll back any other
-- table's already-completed purge in the same run.
--
-- KNOWN LIMITATION, documented rather than hidden: a tenant currently
-- cannot be purged while `php stone generate tenant-governance`'s
-- tenant_memberships.tenant_id -> tenants(uuid) ON DELETE CASCADE tries to
-- cascade-delete its founder's membership row — that row's own
-- trg_protect_tenant_creator trigger unconditionally refuses any hard
-- DELETE of an is_tenant_creator row. See the tenants-specific purge
-- block's EXCEPTION comment for the full explanation; resolving it requires
-- a coordinated change to tenant-governance itself (out of scope here).
--
-- The DELETE is what makes this real: if `php stone generate audit` is
-- installed on a given table, that DELETE fires the AFTER-row trigger and
-- writes the immutable _audit_log proof of erasure in the SAME transaction
-- as the physical delete — the two can never drift apart. If audit is NOT
-- installed on a table, the delete still happens safely, it just leaves no
-- immutable trail — install `php stone generate audit` first if that
-- matters for a given table (it does, for identities/tenants).
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION purge_expired_deletions()
RETURNS TABLE (o_table_name TEXT, o_purged_count INTEGER, o_error TEXT)
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = pg_catalog, public
AS $$
DECLARE
    v_count INTEGER;
    v_deleted_ids TEXT[];
    v_deleted_ats TIMESTAMPTZ[];
BEGIN
__PURGE_TABLE_BLOCKS__
    RETURN;
END;
$$;
